  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${135}^{\u2218}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{135}^{\u2218}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061{135}^{\u2218}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{135}^{\u2218}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
